# Latest stable version for new dependencies and services

<!-- core -->
Before adding any new dependency or service (library, package, image, DB like Postgres or Redis),
look up its current latest stable version. Never use a remembered or training-data version — it is a
guess about the past, and it fails quietly. If the lookup is inconclusive, ask the user to confirm
before proceeding.

<!-- /core -->

## Why the lookup is not optional

A version recalled from training data is a guess about the past. It is routinely several major
versions behind, and the failure is quiet: the build resolves, the code compiles, and the project
starts life on an unsupported release with known CVEs and a migration already owed.

## How to look it up

- Package registry or the project's own releases page for the authoritative current version.
- Context7 (`resolve-library-id`, then `query-docs`) for the docs that go with that version — see
  `~/.claude/rules/context7.md`.
- For Docker images, the registry's tag list. Do not assume two images from the same vendor share a
  release cadence or a tag scheme.

## Stable means stable

Not a release candidate, alpha, beta, nightly or `-SNAPSHOT`, unless the user asked for one or the
project already pins pre-releases deliberately.

## When the lookup is inconclusive

Inconclusive includes: sources disagreeing,
no clear stable line, a project that has forked or been renamed, or a major version whose migration
cost you cannot assess from the release notes.

## Existing dependencies

This rule is about *adding*. Upgrading what is already pinned is a separate decision with its own
blast radius — raise it, do not fold it silently into unrelated work.

Applies globally, all projects, no exceptions.
